     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose looks unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg/day. That is twice the usual maximum of 2,550 mg/day for immediate-release metformin, and more than twice the 2,000 mg/day maximum for extended-release. Doses this high raise the risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity. Normal kidney and liver function don't change that. It may be a typo. For example, 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg/day) or 1,000 mg twice daily would both be within limits.\n\n**2. You're not a prescriber.** Medication orders should come from a licensed clinician with prescribing authority for this patient, or be verified by one.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- If the treating clinician intended a different dose, send me the corrected dose and confirm that they authorized the order. I can enter it then.\n- If the high dose is intentional, the prescriber should confirm it and document why. I'd also want pharmacy to review it before I enter anything."
      }
     ],
